WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Here are three bottles of clear, colourless liquid:

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.080
heptane, octane and nonane.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.440
They're alkane neighbours, and they look identical.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.680
The last video said a longer chain means

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.680
stronger dispersion forces.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:14.960
That's a claim.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.160
So how do you test it in a

00:00:16.281 --> 00:00:18.960
school lab, safely, and how far can you

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.160
trust what you find?

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:25.126
First, choose members of one series with straight

00:00:25.207 --> 00:00:27.606
chains, so the only thing that changes is

00:00:27.687 --> 00:00:30.046
the length, one C H two at a

00:00:30.087 --> 00:00:30.806
time.

00:00:31.127 --> 00:00:33.206
A branched isomer would change the shape as

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:33.606
well.

00:00:34.327 --> 00:00:37.526
Heptane, octane and nonane are all liquids at

00:00:37.607 --> 00:00:38.406
room temperature.

00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:42.406
Methane to butane are gases, and pentane boils

00:00:42.487 --> 00:00:45.086
at thirty six degrees, so its vapour builds

00:00:45.127 --> 00:00:47.606
up fastest, which makes it the biggest fire

00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:48.966
risk in a class test.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:52.886
Next, choose a property.

00:00:53.207 --> 00:00:55.446
Boiling point is the obvious one, but that

00:00:55.527 --> 00:00:58.486
means heating a flammable liquid, and any flame

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:01.766
or hot surface nearby could ignite its vapour.

00:01:01.847 --> 00:01:04.966
So measure something at room temperature: how fast

00:01:05.127 --> 00:01:06.726
each liquid evaporates.

00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:09.606
Only the fastest molecules have enough energy to

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:12.486
escape, so the ones left behind are slower

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.486
on average, and the liquid cools.

00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:17.606
The faster it evaporates, the bigger the temperature

00:01:17.687 --> 00:01:18.166
drop.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.486
Wrap a small sleeve of filter paper around

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.566
the tip of a temperature probe.

00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:26.886
Dip it in the heptane for thirty seconds,

00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:28.726
so the paper soaks up liquid at the

00:01:28.807 --> 00:01:30.406
sample's temperature.

00:01:30.607 --> 00:01:32.806
Then lift it out, and log the temperature

00:01:32.887 --> 00:01:36.006
every couple of seconds until it stops falling.

00:01:36.087 --> 00:01:38.886
The start reading minus the lowest reading is

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:41.206
the temperature drop, delta T.

00:01:41.607 --> 00:01:43.686
Do it in a fume cupboard, three times

00:01:43.847 --> 00:01:44.806
for each alkane.

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:48.433
So what are the variables?

00:01:48.914 --> 00:01:52.113
The independent variable is the number of carbons:

00:01:52.274 --> 00:01:53.873
seven, eight or nine.

00:01:54.354 --> 00:01:56.833
The dependent variable is the temperature drop.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:59.793
And everything else stays the same: the size

00:01:59.834 --> 00:02:02.433
of the paper, the soaking time, the starting

00:02:02.514 --> 00:02:04.833
temperature, and the probe's place in the fume

00:02:04.914 --> 00:02:08.033
cupboard, because moving air speeds up evaporation.

00:02:10.034 --> 00:02:11.233
Here's one run of each.

00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:15.313
Heptane drops fastest, bottoming out at fifteen point

00:02:15.354 --> 00:02:17.793
eight degrees, then warms up again once the

00:02:17.874 --> 00:02:18.833
liquid's gone.

00:02:19.074 --> 00:02:22.753
Octane falls more slowly, to nineteen point five.

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:26.113
Nonane barely moves, to twenty one point one.

00:02:28.134 --> 00:02:29.293
Now the table.

00:02:29.574 --> 00:02:32.453
Heptane's three drops were five point eight, six

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:35.493
point two and six point zero degrees, a

00:02:35.694 --> 00:02:37.573
mean of six point zero.

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:40.773
Octane's mean is two point two, and nonane's

00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:42.933
is just zero point seven.

00:02:43.014 --> 00:02:45.573
Each extra carbon makes the drop smaller.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.320
Why?

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.280
Inside each molecule there are only carbon to

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.720
carbon and carbon to hydrogen covalent bonds, and

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.440
evaporating doesn't break any of them.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.560
Between the molecules there are dispersion forces, and

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.200
each extra C H two adds electrons and

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.080
contact, so those forces get stronger, as the

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.600
last video showed.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.240
Fewer molecules have enough energy to break free

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.920
each second, so nonane evaporates slowest, and cools

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:14.560
least.

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:19.046
Methane to butane can't be tested this way

00:03:19.087 --> 00:03:19.526
at all.

00:03:20.007 --> 00:03:23.446
So extend it with secondary data: boiling points

00:03:23.527 --> 00:03:25.846
measured and published by others, for the first

00:03:26.007 --> 00:03:27.046
nine alkanes.

00:03:27.527 --> 00:03:29.926
Boiling point rises at every step.

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:32.646
But each step is smaller than the last:

00:03:32.807 --> 00:03:35.846
about seventy three degrees from methane to ethane,

00:03:35.887 --> 00:03:38.166
and only about twenty five from octane to

00:03:38.247 --> 00:03:38.806
nonane.

00:03:39.287 --> 00:03:40.646
Describe that pattern.

00:03:41.047 --> 00:03:43.046
The reason goes beyond this course.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:47.466
And the two methods agree: the higher the

00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:50.106
boiling point, the smaller the temperature drop.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.706
Two independent methods pointing the same way make

00:03:53.747 --> 00:03:55.146
a stronger conclusion.

00:03:57.167 --> 00:04:00.046
Validity: does it measure what it's meant to?

00:04:00.687 --> 00:04:02.686
Only if chain length is the one thing

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.326
that changes, so the controls matter.

00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:08.446
Reliability: do the repeats agree?

00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:11.406
Each alkane's drops sit within half a degree

00:04:11.447 --> 00:04:12.406
of each other.

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:15.966
Accuracy depends on the probe reading true temperatures,

00:04:16.047 --> 00:04:18.646
so check it against a calibrated thermometer.

00:04:18.927 --> 00:04:21.646
The ranking matching the published boiling points supports

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:25.326
the conclusion, but that's agreement, not accuracy.

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:29.973
The temperature drop compares how quickly the liquids

00:04:30.054 --> 00:04:30.773
evaporate.

00:04:31.174 --> 00:04:33.333
It isn't a boiling point, and it doesn't

00:04:33.414 --> 00:04:35.093
measure the forces directly.

00:04:35.414 --> 00:04:37.573
Changes in air flow, or in how much

00:04:37.654 --> 00:04:40.453
liquid the paper holds, shift the readings, which

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:41.733
is why you repeat.

00:04:41.974 --> 00:04:45.413
And the conclusion covers straight chains only: branched

00:04:45.534 --> 00:04:46.933
isomers boil lower.

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:50.333
The risk assessment.

00:04:50.614 --> 00:04:54.213
Heptane and octane are highly flammable, and nonane

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:55.573
is flammable.

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.813
Their vapour can drift to a flame, so

00:04:57.894 --> 00:05:00.693
keep flames and hot plates well away.

00:05:00.814 --> 00:05:02.853
Work in a fume cupboard, because breathing the

00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:04.933
vapour can make you drowsy.

00:05:05.014 --> 00:05:07.973
We swapped out hexane, because repeated exposure can

00:05:08.134 --> 00:05:09.493
damage nerves.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:12.853
Wear safety glasses, keep small volumes in stoppered

00:05:12.894 --> 00:05:16.053
tubes, and pour leftovers into the labelled organic

00:05:16.134 --> 00:05:18.453
waste container, never the sink.

00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:22.946
A short way to keep the result: longer

00:05:23.147 --> 00:05:24.546
chain, smaller chill.

00:05:25.027 --> 00:05:28.706
More carbons mean stronger dispersion forces, so slower

00:05:28.787 --> 00:05:31.666
evaporation, and a smaller temperature drop.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:34.306
Your turn.

00:05:34.787 --> 00:05:37.106
The table gives the normal boiling points of

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:39.026
four straight-chain alkenes.

00:05:39.507 --> 00:05:42.066
Describe the trend, and explain it with reference

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:45.426
to the intramolecular and intermolecular bonding present.

00:05:45.987 --> 00:05:47.346
Pause here to answer.

00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:01.286
Boiling point rises with chain length, by a

00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:04.726
smaller step each time: about thirty six, then

00:06:04.807 --> 00:06:07.046
thirty four, then thirty degrees.

00:06:07.607 --> 00:06:11.286
Inside each molecule, the covalent bonds stay unbroken.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:15.046
Between molecules, each extra C H two adds

00:06:15.127 --> 00:06:19.126
electrons and contact, so dispersion forces get stronger,

00:06:19.207 --> 00:06:21.366
and more energy is needed to separate them.

00:06:21.927 --> 00:06:25.286
"Longer chains have stronger covalent bonds" misses the

00:06:25.367 --> 00:06:26.006
point.

00:06:28.034 --> 00:06:28.753
One more.

00:06:29.234 --> 00:06:31.153
A student plans to find the effect of

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:34.913
chain length on volatility by heating pentane, hexane

00:06:35.074 --> 00:06:38.193
and two-methylpentane in an open test tube over

00:06:38.234 --> 00:06:41.233
a Bunsen burner, and recording each boiling point.

00:06:41.794 --> 00:06:44.913
Evaluate the validity and safety of this plan.

00:06:45.234 --> 00:06:46.513
Pause here to answer.

00:06:57.734 --> 00:07:00.453
The plan can't isolate chain length, and it

00:07:00.534 --> 00:07:01.613
isn't safe.

00:07:01.894 --> 00:07:05.253
Two-methylpentane is branched, so it changes shape as

00:07:05.334 --> 00:07:06.773
well as chain length.

00:07:06.814 --> 00:07:09.493
And an open flame near highly flammable vapour

00:07:09.654 --> 00:07:12.613
could start a fire, especially with pentane, which

00:07:12.694 --> 00:07:15.013
boils at thirty six degrees.

00:07:15.294 --> 00:07:18.213
Instead, compare straight chains by evaporation in a

00:07:18.294 --> 00:07:21.573
fume cupboard, or use published boiling points.

00:07:21.654 --> 00:07:23.573
A smaller flame doesn't fix it.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.440
So: choose straight-chain members of one series, test

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:32.400
a property you can measure without flames, keep

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.000
everything but chain length the same, repeat, and

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.240
back it up with published data.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.880
Then explain the trend with the forces between

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.240
molecules, not the bonds inside them.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.960
Next: what happens when a double bond meets

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:47.920
a reagent.

00:07:49.934 --> 00:07:51.373
Here are the key notes.

00:07:51.614 --> 00:07:53.533
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:53.614 --> 00:07:54.413
to copy them.
